# Action requests and results

A button expresses an intention. Its result needs separate evidence. This contract covers single entities, room actions, scenes and scripts without inventing transactional guarantees across devices.

**HA-CTRL-002 — reference design specification.** This specifies the local design coverage requested by HA-GAP-002 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples do not install a native adapter, connect to a backend or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Before submission | Required decision |
| --- | --- |
| Named target and supported action | Resolve the intended entities through the native target contract; show the affected scope. |
| Confirmation condition | Record whether confirmation is an entity report, task response or integration-specific event. A resolved call alone is not physical proof. |
| Retry characteristics | Establish whether repeated requests are safe, additive or potentially conflicting. |
| Cancellation support | Expose Cancel or Stop only if the underlying operation supports the stated effect. |
| Concurrent actor | Accept that another user, automation or physical control may change the device while this request is pending. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

| State of this operation | Presentation |
| --- | --- |
| Ready | A specific action label and the latest reported device state. |
| Sending or accepted | Pending feedback; the device state stays separately qualified. Prevent accidental duplicate submission for that operation. |
| Observed result | Report the new state and its evidence; do not imply that an unrelated update was caused by this request. |
| Rejected request | Explain the supplied error and a supported recovery route. Keep the current device state. |
| Waiting limit reached | “Not confirmed”; explain that the action may still complete. A timeout is not a rollback. |
| Partial group completion | Give counts and per-member outcomes; retain unknown members. Do not mark the group successful. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Use “Turn off study lights” for the action and “2 lights reported off” for an observed result. A scene activation time or a script run ending does not establish that every target reached the desired physical state. Do not label a scene “On” unless the relevant entity contract supplies that meaning. Show error details only to an appropriate audience; private identifiers should not leak into shared-screen toasts.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Keep a focused button in place while pending. Announce one relevant change through a status region, then retain a visible result. Re-enable the appropriate control after an error without moving focus to a distant toast. A group outcome needs a structured list or table, with a keyboard route to each affected member. Colour is supplemental.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

Preserve native dashboard actions, More info, confirmation and protected-action flows. The theme must not globally wrap every service call or infer completion from a generic promise. The maintained lighting card is a separate, bounded implementation; these examples do not extend it to all domains. If cancellation is unavailable, offer dismissal of the message with clear wording, not a fictional cancellation of the device operation.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-examples">Reference examples</h2>

These complete, isolated HTML specimens use synthetic data. Buttons and forms only demonstrate local feedback or draft validation. Static state examples are labelled as such; no action reaches a household.

<%= example('ha-action-request') %>

<%= example('ha-action-pending') %>

<%= example('ha-action-partial') %>

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-002-acceptance">Acceptance and evidence</h2>

1. Resolve a request before the state update, and state-update before the response. Neither ordering may create duplicate announcements or premature success.
2. Exercise group outcomes containing confirmed, rejected, unavailable and unconfirmed members. Counts must reconcile with the intended targets.
3. Let an operation finish after the waiting limit and after its message is dismissed. Update from current evidence and do not repeat it automatically.
4. Change the same device from a second session while pending. Keep native state authoritative and retain explicit distinctions between Cancel, Stop and Dismiss.

Record the integration/frontend version, browser, fixture or device, input state, expected result, observed result and evidence location. Source review and documentation checks are not live-device verification. Record unresolved native limitations explicitly; do not turn this checklist into an accessibility guarantee.

